            <ArticleTitle>Robotic-assisted bladder neck repair: feasibility and outcomes.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>111-20</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ucl.2014.09.013</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0094-0143(14)00109-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Minimally invasive techniques are rapidly being developed and integrated into urologic surgery. Over the past 5 years, the urologic literature is abound with novel techniques and adaptations to conventional laparoscopy. Pediatric urology is no exception to this trend, and the benefits of minimally invasive surgery may be accentuated in children given the relatively more confined working spaces and also a heightened awareness of cosmesis for the pediatric population. Increasingly, complex pediatric urologic procedures are being performed with robot assistance. The feasibility of nephrectomy, pyeloplasty, ureteral reimplantation, and bladder surgery has been clearly established.</AbstractText>
